.TH CJSON_SET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_set \- write a C scalar to the DOM leaf at a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_set(cjson root, const char *path, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Expands to a call to _cjson_set_typed() with the type and raw value
of val derived at compile time via _Generic.

Accepted C types for val:
  bool                       -> CJSON_BOOL
  any integer type           -> CJSON_INTEGER (stored as long long)
  float / double             -> CJSON_FLOAT  (stored as double)
  char * / const char *      -> CJSON_STRING  (strdup'd copy)
  string literal ("...")     -> CJSON_STRING
  NULL                       -> CJSON_NULL

Any other C type (long double, a struct, an enum, or any pointer
type other than char * / const char *) is rejected: cjson_set
returns ccol_invalid_args and the target leaf is left untouched
(created leaves are not left behind as CJSON_NULL for an unsupported
type; an existing leaf's value is not overwritten).

A void * value that is not NULL is rejected the same way
(ccol_invalid_args), rather than being silently written as
CJSON_NULL: only a genuine NULL value is treated as an intentional
null, even though void * is also the compile-time type of the bare
NULL literal.

Signed integer types are sign-extended to long long correctly.
Unsigned integers in the range [0, LLONG_MAX] round-trip faithfully.
Unsigned values > LLONG_MAX are stored with their bit pattern
reinterpreted as a signed long long.

The leaf is created if it does not yet exist (its immediate parent
must already exist).  If the leaf exists its type is changed
unconditionally: existing array or object subtrees are deep-freed
before the new value is written.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root   Root cjson node.
path   Dot-separated path string to the target leaf.
       Escape sequences: '\e.' -> literal '.', '\e\e' -> literal '\e'.
       A '\e' before any other character is left unchanged.
val    C value; the type must be one of the accepted types listed above.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t: ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args; an empty path or path component (leading,
                     trailing, or consecutive dots), parent type
                     incompatible with the leaf component, a
                     malformed array index (non-numeric, negative,
                     or bare '#' with no digits), val's C type is
                     not one of the accepted types listed above, or
                     val is a void * value that is not NULL.
ccol_key_not_found; the parent path, or a syntactically valid but
                     out-of-range array index, does not exist.  An
                     array, unlike an object, has no way to
                     auto-extend to fit an arbitrary index.
ccol_not_enough_memory; allocation failure.
ccol_container_full; the parent object has already reached its
                     maximum element count (creating a new key only).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Passing a bool literal requires an explicit cast: (bool)true.
Without the cast, the integer value 1 is seen instead of _Bool,
resulting in CJSON_INTEGER rather than CJSON_BOOL.

Plain char inherits the platform's default signedness (signed on
x86/x86-64 Linux, unsigned where -funsigned-char applies, e.g. ARM).
Use signed char or unsigned char explicitly when portability matters.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.active", (bool)true);
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.score",  99);
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.label",  "champion");
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.ratio",  0.75);

/* Type change: replace an existing object subtree with a scalar */
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.metadata", 0);  /* deep-frees old subtree */

/* Set to null */
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.deprecated", NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_get (3),
.BR _cjson_set_typed (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_set (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3)
